6. a) Use ArrayList to store student objects. 

Aim:
To create and use an ArrayList in Java to store and display student objects.

PROGRAM :- 

import java.util.ArrayList;
class Student {
    int rollNo;
    String name;
    Student(int rollNo, String name) {
        this.rollNo = rollNo;
        this.name = name;
    }
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Roll No: " + rollNo);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println();
    }
}
public class StudentArrayList {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<Student>();
        students.add(new Student(101, "Rahul"));
        students.add(new Student(102, "Priya"));
        students.add(new Student(103, "Amit"));
        System.out.println("Student Details:");
        for (Student s : students) {
            s.display();
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Student Details:

Roll No: 101
Name: Rahul

Roll No: 102
Name: Priya

Roll No: 103
Name: Amit


6. b) Perform add, remove, search operations. 

Aim:
To perform add, remove, and search operations using ArrayList in Java.

Program :- 

import java.util.ArrayList;
public class ArrayListOperations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> students = new ArrayList<String>();

        // Add elements
        students.add("Rahul");
        students.add("Priya");
        students.add("Amit");
        System.out.println("Students after adding:");
        System.out.println(students);

        // Remove an element
        students.remove("Priya");
        System.out.println("Students after removing Priya:");
        System.out.println(students);

        // Search an element
        if (students.contains("Amit")) {
            System.out.println("Amit is found.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Amit is not found.");
        }
    }
}

Output :-

Students after adding:
[Rahul, Priya, Amit]
Students after removing Priya:
[Rahul, Amit]
Amit is found.

6.c) Use HashSet to store unique elements. 

Aim:
To create and use a HashSet in Java to store unique elements.

PROGRAM :- 

import java.util.HashSet;
public class HashSetDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashSet<String> names = new HashSet<String>();
        // Add elements
        names.add("Rahul");
        names.add("Priya");
        names.add("Amit");
        names.add("Rahul");   // Duplicate element
        System.out.println("Unique Elements:");
        System.out.println(names);
    }
}

Output :-

Unique Elements:
[Rahul, Priya, Amit]


6.d) Iterate collections using Iterator and for-each loop.

Aim:
To iterate through a collection using Iterator and for-each loop in Java.

PROGRAM :- 

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Iterator;
public class IteratorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();
        names.add("Rahul");
        names.add("Priya");
        names.add("Amit");
        // Using Iterator
        System.out.println("Using Iterator:");
        Iterator<String> it = names.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            System.out.println(it.next());
        }
        // Using For-Each Loop
        System.out.println("\nUsing For-Each Loop:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Using Iterator:
Rahul
Priya
Amit
Using For-Each Loop:
Rahul
Priya
Amit

